## 2. Three key changes

### Change 1: Tencent "reported small accounts" for the first time - core business profit margin 43%

This is the most important new information of the season. For the first time, Tencent has separated its investment in new AI products (Hunyuan, Yuanbao, CodeBuddy, WorkBuddy, QClaw) from its core business:

| Caliber | Q1 2026 | Q1 2025 | Year-on-year | Profit margin |
|------|---------|---------|------|--------|
| Non-IFRS operating profit (including AI products) | 75.6 billion | 69.3 billion | +9% | 38.5% |
| **Non-IFRS operating profit (excluding AI products)** | **84.4 billion** | **72.1 billion** | **+17%** | **43.0%** |
| AI new product loss | **About 8.8 billion** | About 2.8 billion | +214% | — |

Seeing this set of numbers, you may have the same intuition as me - Tencent is "reporting a small account."

On the surface, profits only increased by 9%, but the profit growth rate of the core business (games + advertising + financial technology) was 17%, and the profit margin was 43%. The so-called "growth slowdown" is entirely due to the 8.8 billion spent on AI products in the first quarter.

In a word: It’s not that Tencent can’t make money anymore, it’s that it’s using the money it makes to feed AI. 8.8 billion were fed in the first quarter.

### Change 2: Advertising business accelerates to +20%, AI money is not wasted

The growth rate of marketing services revenue increased to 20% from 17% in the previous quarter, which was the fastest growth rate among all sectors.

The growth comes from two aspects: the video account user duration +20% year-on-year has brought more advertising inventory (volume), and the AI-driven advertising recommendation model upgrade has improved advertising effects and pricing capabilities (price). The AIM+ automated delivery solution has covered approximately 30% of advertiser expenditures. The specific contribution of "volume" and "price" was not disclosed in the financial report.

Advertising is the most intuitive case of AI empowering existing businesses - investment in AI is not all about "burning money", and part of it is already making money.

### Change three: Repurchase "ebbs" - where does the money go?

In the past two years, Tencent has relied on buybacks to send "red envelopes" to shareholders. Now the red envelope budget has been diverted to recruit AI engineers and buy GPUs.

Buybacks have slowed down significantly from HK$112 billion in 2024 to about HK$80 billion in 2025, to HK$7.6 billion in Q1 2026 (annualized to about HK$30 billion). At the same time, capital expenditures jumped to 31.9 billion (+16%) from 27.5 billion in Q1 last year, a 63% increase from the previous quarter.

The good news is: Even with reduced buybacks and increased capital expenditures, Tencent's net cash increased from 90.2 billion to 146.9 billion (+63%). Not only was there no less cash in my pocket, but it was even more. Free cash flow was 56.7 billion, +20% year-on-year - making money faster than spending money.

Liu Chiping's tone at the performance meeting was: **In 2026, "revenue growth may be faster than profit growth"** - This is to manage market expectations in advance, which means that AI will continue to burn, and don't expect too high profit growth rates.

The risk is also clear: if AI fails to make a mark in a few years, the money that could have been spent on repurchases will be wasted. This plan is not cost-effective. We will not know until 3 years later.

