## 4. An easily overlooked detail

The growth rate of accounts receivable (+16%) is significantly faster than the growth rate of revenue (+9%). It needs to be observed in Q2 whether it is collected normally. The possible reason is that large customers in the rapid growth of advertising and cloud businesses have longer billing periods, but if they continue to expand, they need to be vigilant.

---

## 5. Conclusion: What has changed in this financial report?

**On the surface, it is lower than expected, but in reality, it is slightly higher. **

The revenue loss of 2.5 billion is mainly due to the delayed recognition of game revenue due to the misalignment of the Spring Festival. This is an old problem and will naturally be made up for in Q2. What really matters is that the profit data "excluding AI products" disclosed for the first time - core business +17% growth, 43% profit margin - are far better than market concerns.

**For the holder: Hold, no action required. **

The money-making machine of the core business is turning well. Spending money on AI is a proactive move, not a forced one. Net cash of RMB 146.9 billion provides sufficient safety cushion. The only thing you need to be vigilant about is: if there is no improvement in AI product user data for 2-3 consecutive quarters, you need to re-evaluate the return expectations on AI investment.

**Next thing to watch**:
- Q2 financial report - game revenue to make up for the Spring Festival time difference + whether the scale of AI product losses will continue to expand
- The official version of Hunyuan 3 - whether external developers can be retained after the limited exemption period ends
- Yuanbao user growth - after peaking during the Spring Festival, it fell back to 57 million. Can it regain momentum?

---

**Summary of this financial report in one sentence**: The core business is rock solid, AI investment is burning money fiercely, and the ebb in buybacks is regrettable.

Investing in Tencent is essentially betting on one thing: whether this company can create a "WeChat-level" product in the AI era. The answer to Q1 is: not yet, but it can afford the money to try. In Ma Huateng's own words - the boat stood on board, but he still couldn't sit down.

